# Summary of llama.cpp Backend Documentation

This document provides a comprehensive overview of various hardware acceleration backends supported by llama.cpp for running large language model inference across diverse platforms including Android GPUs, CANN NPUs, OpenCL devices Intel SYCL/oneAPI accelerators OpenVINO optimized processors AMD ZenDNN CPUs Qualcomm Hexagon/NPU devices and AMD zDNN mainframes along with cross-platform virtualized GPU solutions. Each section details setup procedures operational constraints performance characteristics environment variable configurations known limitations active development roadmaps testing methodologies architectural design choices edge case handling mechanisms developer contribution guidelines troubleshooting steps verification protocols build automation workflows containerization strategies deployment patterns multi-device execution modes partitioning strategies memory management paradigms kernel optimization techniques tracing instrumentation requirements cache alignment rules thread distribution models fallback behaviors workload balancing algorithms tensor parallelism implementations row splitting layer splitting device grouping semantics unified address space window constraints dynamic buffer mapping sliding window allocation host side precomputation kernel parameter packing trace event generation profiling counter collection post processing script integration environment configuration key value mappings hypervisor capset routing venus versus apir capability sets virglrenderer remoting protocol hypercall interfaces shared memory buffer layouts zero copy data transfer mechanisms communication layer abstractions backend library loading function symbol resolution dynamic registration callbacks runtime initialization sequences default behavior overrides debugging logging file output paths error message diagnostics recovery procedures prerequisite software dependency chains compiler toolchain installations driver firmware updates operating system package managers repo